  "hints": [
    "Define `best_ending_here` as the best streak that ends exactly at day `i`. How does it relate to the same value for day `i-1`?",
    "Either the best streak ending at `i` is just day `i` alone, or it extends the best streak ending at `i-1`.",
    "Keep two numbers while scanning: the best ending here and the best overall. Initialise from the first element, not from 0, so all-negative inputs work."
  ],
  "editorial": "Let `cur` be the best sum of a streak that **ends at the current day**. Either it starts fresh at this day (`x`) or it extends the previous best streak (`cur + x`), so `cur = max(x, cur + x)`. The answer is the maximum `cur` seen. This is one pass, `O(n)` time and `O(1)` memory.\n\nTraps: initialising the answer to `0` is wrong when every value is negative (the streak must be non-empty), and totals reach `2·10^5 · 10^9 = 2·10^14` at real scale, so use 64-bit integers in C++ and Java.\n\nThe brute force tries every `(i, j)` pair in `O(n^2)` with prefix sums, which is what the browser-sized limit allows; the linear scan is the intended solution.",
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n)",
    "space": "O(1)"
  },
  "starter": {
    "python": "import sys\n\n# Print the maximum sum of a non-empty contiguous subarray.\ndata = sys.stdin.read().split()\n# your code here\n",
    "javascript": "// Print the maximum sum of a non-empty contiguous subarray.\n// readLine() returns the next line of input (or null).\n// your code here\n",
    "cpp": "#include <bits/stdc++.h>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    // Print the maximum sum of a non-empty contiguous subarray.\n    // your code here\n}\n",
    "java": "import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner in = new Scanner(System.in);\n        // Print the maximum sum of a non-empty contiguous subarray.\n        // your code here\n    }\n}\n"
